&ldquo;Hand the stuns&rsquo;ls!&nbsp; Hang out every rag
            you&rsquo;ve got!&nbsp; Clothe her from stem to
            rudder-post!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Amidships! amidships, you&mdash;! <a name="citation9"></a><a
                href="#footnote9" class="citation">[9]</a> or I&rsquo;ll brain the last idiot of
            you!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Very well, then, let his boarders freeze till the next
            comet comes.&nbsp; Lighten ship!&nbsp; Lively, now, lively,
            men!&nbsp; Heave the whole cargo overboard!&rdquo;

&ldquo;Ta-ta! ta-ta!&nbsp; Maybe <i>you&rsquo;ve</i> got some
            message to send your friends in the Everlasting
            Tropics!&rdquo;

I lit.&nbsp; I drifted up to a gate with a swarm of people,
            and when it was my turn the head clerk says, in a business-like
            way&mdash;

By George, Peters, think of it!&nbsp;
            &ldquo;<i>Planet</i>?&rdquo; says I; &ldquo;it&rsquo;s a
            city.&nbsp; And moreover, it&rsquo;s one of the biggest and
            finest and&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;There, there!&rdquo; says he, &ldquo;no time here for
            conversation.&nbsp; We don&rsquo;t deal in cities here.&nbsp;
            Where are you from in a <i>general</i> way?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, my goodness!&rdquo; says I.&nbsp;
            &ldquo;Constellation, says you?&nbsp; No&mdash;it&rsquo;s a
            State.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Man, we don&rsquo;t deal in States here.&nbsp;
            <i>Will</i> you tell me where you are from <i>in general&mdash;at
                large</i>, don&rsquo;t you understand?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Oh, now I get your idea,&rdquo; I says.&nbsp;
            &ldquo;I&rsquo;m from America,&mdash;the United States of
            America.&rdquo;

&ldquo;<i>Orb</i>?&rdquo; says I.&nbsp; &ldquo;Why, what are
            you talking about, young man?&nbsp; It ain&rsquo;t an orb;
            it&rsquo;s a country; it&rsquo;s a continent.&nbsp; Columbus
            discovered it; I reckon likely you&rsquo;ve heard of <i>him</i>,
            anyway.&nbsp; America&mdash;why, sir, America&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well,&rdquo; says I, &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know anything
            more to say&mdash;unless I lump things, and just say I&rsquo;m
            from the world.&rdquo;

Peters, he had <i>me</i>, that time.&nbsp; I looked at him,
            puzzled, he looked at me, worried.&nbsp; Then he burst
            out&mdash;

&ldquo;<i>The</i> world!&rdquo; he says.&nbsp;
            &ldquo;H&rsquo;m! there&rsquo;s billions of them! . . .
            Next!&rdquo;

&ldquo;That is not true; and this is not the place for
            prevarication.&nbsp; You wandered from your course.&nbsp; How did
            that happen?&rdquo;

Says I to myself, &ldquo;Young man, it wouldn&rsquo;t be
            wholesome for you to go down <i>there</i> and call it the
            Wart.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I beg pardon, and you mustn&rsquo;t mind my reminding
            you, and seeming to meddle, but hain&rsquo;t you forgot
            something?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I had that idea&mdash;but I see the foolishness
            of it.&nbsp; Which way am I to go to get to my
            district?&rdquo;

He called the under clerk that had examined the map, and he
            gave me general directions.&nbsp; I thanked him and started; but
            he says&mdash;

&ldquo;Wait a minute; it is millions of leagues from
            here.&nbsp; Go outside and stand on that red wishing-carpet; shut
            your eyes, hold your breath, and wish yourself there.&rdquo;

I hopped onto the carpet and held my breath and shut my eyes
            and wished I was in the booking-office of my own section.&nbsp;
            The very next instant a voice I knew sung out in a business kind
            of a way&mdash;

&ldquo;A harp and a hymn-book, pair of wings and a halo, size
            13, for Cap&rsquo;n Eli Stormfield, of San Francisco!&mdash;make
            him out a clean bill of health, and let him in.&rdquo;

We begun to meet swarms of folks who were coming back.&nbsp;
            Some had harps and nothing else; some had hymn-books and nothing
            else; some had nothing at all; all of them looked meek and
            uncomfortable; one young fellow hadn&rsquo;t anything left but
            his halo, and he was carrying that in his hand; all of a sudden
            he offered it to me and says&mdash;

Says I, &ldquo;Old man, I&rsquo;ll be frank with you.&nbsp;
            This <i>ain&rsquo;t</i> just as near my idea of bliss as I
            thought it was going to be, when I used to go to
            church.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Now that&rsquo;s all reasonable and right,&rdquo; says
            I.&nbsp; &ldquo;Plenty of work, and the kind you hanker after; no
            more pain, no more suffering&mdash;&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well,&rdquo; says I, &ldquo;come to think,
            there&rsquo;s something just here that I want to ask about.&nbsp;
            Down below, I always had an idea that in heaven we would all be
            young, and bright, and spry.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I did.&nbsp; They all do.&nbsp; You&rsquo;ll try it,
            some day, like enough; but you&rsquo;ll get tired of the change
            pretty soon.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I&rsquo;ll tell you.&nbsp; Now you&rsquo;ve
            always been a sailor; did you ever try some other
            business?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes, I tried keeping grocery, once, up in the mines;
            but I couldn&rsquo;t stand it; it was too dull&mdash;no stir, no
            storm, no life about it; it was like being part dead and part
            alive, both at the same time.&nbsp; I wanted to be one thing or
            t&rsquo;other.&nbsp; I shut up shop pretty quick and went to
            sea.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well,&rdquo; says I, &ldquo;do you mean to say
            you&rsquo;re going to stand still at seventy-two,
            forever?&rdquo;

&ldquo;No, they&rsquo;ll come together and get adjusted by and
            by.&nbsp; But not this year, and not next.&nbsp; By and
            by.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Yes,&rdquo; he says, in a dry sort of way, &ldquo;they
            mostly go to the wash&mdash;about this time&mdash;I&rsquo;ve
            often noticed it.&nbsp; Fresh angels are powerful neat.&nbsp;
            When do you look for &rsquo;em back?&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well,&rdquo; says I, &ldquo;I&rsquo;ve tucked mine away
            in the cupboard, and I allow to let them lay there till
            there&rsquo;s mud.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, you can see one to-night if you want to.&nbsp;
            There&rsquo;s a barkeeper from Jersey City going to be
            received.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That&rsquo;s all right.&nbsp; You&rsquo;ll find
            they&rsquo;ve been raked up and saved for you.&nbsp; Send for
            them.&rdquo;

&ldquo;I&rsquo;ll do it, Sandy.&nbsp; But what was it you was
            saying about unsacrilegious things, which people expect to get,
            and will be disappointed about?&rdquo;

Sandy sent home for his things, and I sent for mine, and about
            nine in the evening we begun to dress.&nbsp; Sandy
            says,&mdash;

&ldquo;Who&mdash;<i>I</i>?&nbsp; Why, what can you be thinking
            about, Stormy?&nbsp; I ain&rsquo;t worthy to speak to such as
            they.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Could Tom, Dick and Harry call on the Cabinet of Russia
            and do that?&mdash;on Prince Gortschakoff, for
            instance?&rdquo;

&ldquo;I get your idea, Sandy.&nbsp; He couldn&rsquo;t
            interest me.&nbsp; He would be an ignoramus in such
            things&mdash;he would bore me, and I would bore him.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Well, I judge I did.&nbsp; It was the talk of these
            realms&mdash;not for a day, like this barkeeper business, but for
            twenty years before the man died.&rdquo;

All of a sudden the whole region fairly rocked under the crash
            of eleven hundred and one thunder blasts, all let off at once,
            and Sandy says,&mdash;

&ldquo;No, let it slide, Sandy, I don&rsquo;t mind.&nbsp; But
            you&rsquo;ve got a Sandy Hook <i>here</i>, too, have
            you?&rdquo;

&ldquo;How do we know there&rsquo;s eleven hundred and one,
            Sandy, when they all go off at once?&mdash;and yet we certainly
            do know.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t mention it, Sandy,&rdquo; says I, coloring
            up a little; &ldquo;I wouldn&rsquo;t have had the family see it
            for any amount you are a mind to name.&nbsp; Change the subject,
            Sandy, change the subject.&rdquo;

&ldquo;We&rsquo;ll sit down here and wait.&nbsp; We&rsquo;ll
            see the head of the procession come in sight away off yonder
            pretty soon, now.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Don&rsquo;t you fret, it&rsquo;s all right.&nbsp;
            There&rsquo;ll be one more gun-fire&mdash;then you&rsquo;ll
            see.&rdquo;

&ldquo;Keep your eyes on the Grand Stand and the miles of
            seats&mdash;sharp!&rdquo; says Sandy, &ldquo;and listen for the
            gun-fire.&rdquo;

&ldquo;That is the way we do it here.&nbsp; No time fooled
            away; nobody straggling in after the curtain&rsquo;s up.&nbsp;
            Wishing is quicker work than travelling.&nbsp; A quarter of a
            second ago these folks were millions of miles from here.&nbsp;
            When they heard the last signal, all they had to do was to wish,
            and here they are.&rdquo;
